ion polling available"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point firmly to Sessions: a ten-point Republican district, a well-funded incumbent, and a Democratic challenger who has raised under $9,000. Every expert rater places the race in the Safe or Solid Republican column, and markets price a Democratic win at about five percent, so the forecast sits near the rating consensus at roughly thirteen points Republican.", "watch": "Any late surge in Democratic fundraising or outside spending, or a polling error well beyond the historical norm in Texas, would be needed to move this race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Pete Sessions (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Pete Sessions, the Republican incumbent, is seeking another term in a Central Texas district that leans about ten points Republican. His Democratic challenger, Waco attorney Casey Shepard, has run a minimal campaign, reporting only a few thousand dollars raised against Sessions's roughly $1 million war chest and no outside support. Sessions has also drawn positive local coverage for federal funding projects in the district.\n\nNo public general-election polls are available, so the outlook rests on district fundamentals, the fundraising gap, and expert assessments. All ten ratings place the race as Safe or Solid Republican, and prediction markets give Democrats only a small chance. The district's new map was drawn to be somewhat more competitive, but the evidence still points to a comfortable Sessions win.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0355, "margin": -14.61}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0379, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.031, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0515, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0249, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0237, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0293, "margin": -15